    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3585",
      "problem": "For the function $y=f(x)$ below, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/1aeebec4df41080efc0d91a8adf00a799828e793e731ed7a36d32d881110596b.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote.",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/observations/step-e3585-329e995ef39ed2830657.html",
